$\begin{align}
\sin(x)+\cos(y)&=\sqrt{2}
\\\\
\cos(x)\cdot\dfrac{dx}{dt}-\sin(y)\cdot\dfrac{dy}{dt}&=0
\end{align}$

We are given that $\goldD{\dfrac{dx}{dt}=5}$, and we want to find $\dfrac{dy}{dt}$ when $\blueD{y=\dfrac{\pi}{4}}$.

We also need to find the corresponding value of $\maroonD{x}$. To do that, we can plug $\blueD{y=\dfrac{\pi}{4}}$ into the relating equation:

$\begin{align}
\sin(\maroonD{x})+\cos(\blueD{y})&=\sqrt{2}
\\\\
\sin(\maroonD{x})+\cos\left(\blueD{\dfrac{\pi}{4}}\right)&=\sqrt{2}
\\\\
\sin(\maroonD{x})+\dfrac{\sqrt{2}}{2}&=\sqrt{2}
\\\\
\sin(\maroonD{x})&=\dfrac{\sqrt{2}}{2}
\\\\
\maroonD{x}&\maroonD{=\dfrac{\pi}{4}}
\end{align}$
